# Production reverse proxy. {$DOMAIN} must be a real, publicly
# resolvable domain name pointing at this host - Caddy then handles HTTPS
# automatically (Let's Encrypt acquisition + renewal + HTTP->HTTPS
# redirect), no extra config needed.
#
# WebSocket upgrades (the app's /ws endpoint) are proxied transparently by
# reverse_proxy with no special directive required.
#
# Local testing: setting DOMAIN=localhost makes Caddy use its internal
# (self-signed) CA instead of attempting real Let's Encrypt issuance - see
# docs/deployment.md's "Local HTTPS validation" section.
#
# Multi-replica load balancing: `reverse_proxy web:8000` (a single,
# literal upstream address) was verified LIVE to NOT load-balance across
# `docker compose up --scale web=N` replicas - Caddy resolves a plain
# hostname upstream once and keeps using that one resolved address for
# the life of the process, so every request kept landing on the same
# replica no matter how many were healthy (in a live reproduction,
# 20/20 requests hit replica 1, 0/20 hit replica 2). `dynamic a` is
# Caddy's own built-in DNS-based dynamic-upstream source: it re-resolves the `web` service name every
# `refresh` interval and treats EVERY A record Docker's embedded DNS
# returns (one per healthy replica) as a separate load-balanced upstream -
# this is what actually makes `--scale web=N` traffic spread across all N
# replicas, still using only the existing Caddy already in this stack (no
# new proxy/platform). `refresh 5s` keeps that view of "which replicas
# exist" reasonably current for a scale-up/scale-down without re-resolving
# on literally every single request. `lb_policy round_robin` is explicit
# (rather than relying on Caddy's `random` default) so traffic
# distribution across replicas is deterministic and easy to verify.
{$DOMAIN} {
	reverse_proxy {
		dynamic a web 8000 {
			refresh 5s
		}
		lb_policy round_robin
	}
}
